    case = {'id': 'k1', 'title': 'A minister\'s briefing note on three decades of reform', 'difficulty': 'hard',
            'case': ('A briefing note, using figures from the study material, says: "In 1991 our reserves were only US$1.2 billion. We cut the top tariff from 355% in 1990-91 to 85% by 1993-94 and devalued the rupee by about 18%. '
                     'Licensing now applies to only five industries. Growth, which averaged 3.5% during 1950–80, reached about 8.85% in 1999 and, after a fall of about 6.6% in 2020, rebounded to about 8.68% in 2021. Public debt was about 86% of GDP in FY21/22. The Planning Commission was replaced on 1 January 2015."'),
            'mcqs': [
                M('k10a', 'By how many percentage points did growth swing from 2020 to 2021, as per the note?', ['15.28', '2.08', '8.68', '6.6'], 0, "Swing = 2021 growth − 2020 growth = 8.68 − (−6.60) = 8.68 + 6.60 = 15.28 percentage points. The tempting 2.08 forgets that 2020 growth was negative (8.68 − 6.60); 8.68 and 6.6 are the individual figures. In practice: analysts describe such swings as 'base effects', since a rebound after a sharp fall can look very large.", 'c6', 'hard', 'round(8.68-(-6.6), 2)', topic='The Era of Reforms'),
                M('k10b', 'The relative fall in the top tariff from 355% to 85% is approximately:', ['76.06%', '270%', '23.94%', '85%'], 0, 'Relative fall = (355 − 85) ÷ 355 × 100 = 270 ÷ 355 × 100 ≈ 76.06%. The tempting 270% is the fall in percentage points, not a relative percentage; 23.94% is the new rate as a share of the old (85 ÷ 355); 85% is the new rate itself. In practice: precise language — percentage points versus percent — matters when CAs and economists write budget commentaries.', 'c5', 'hard', 'round((355-85)/355*100, 2)', topic='The Era of Reforms'),
                M('k10c', 'Which of the following still requires an industrial licence, as per the material?', ['Narcotic drugs and hazardous chemicals', 'Automobiles', 'Steel', 'Cement'], 0, 'Per the material, five industries still need a licence — arms and ammunition; atomic substances; narcotics and hazardous chemicals; alcoholic drinks; and cigarettes and cigars. Narcotic drugs and hazardous chemicals are on the list. Automobiles, steel and cement are traps — all were delicensed after 1991. In practice: new projects in chemical and pharmaceutical manufacturing must check whether they handle licensed hazardous substances.', 'c5', 'moderate', topic='The Era of Reforms'),
                M('k10d', 'The body that replaced the Planning Commission is mainly criticised for:', ['Having no role in the budget process', 'Controlling state budgets', 'Running railways', 'Fixing exchange rates'], 0, "The body that replaced the Planning Commission on 1 January 2015 is NITI Aayog. Its main criticism is that it has no role in the budget process and so cannot direct resources the way the Planning Commission did. 'Controlling state budgets' is the trap — it has no such power. In practice: NITI Aayog influences policy mainly through research, indices and advice to ministries and States.", 'c7', 'moderate', topic='NITI Aayog: a Bold Step for Transforming India'),
                M('k10e', 'The debt figure in the note compares with the EMDE average (IMF, 2022) of:', ['64.5%', '86%', '46%', '100%'], 0, "Per the material, the EMDE average debt in 2022 (IMF) was 64.5% of GDP, against India's 86% in FY21/22. The tempting 86% is India's own figure; 46% is a distractor. In practice: sovereign-rating analysts compare India's debt with peer averages like this, and the gap is a key reason for India's fiscal-consolidation targets.", 'c6', 'moderate', '64.5', topic='The Era of Reforms'),
            ]}

